{
  "segment_function": "To analyze Arendt's concept of world alienation and its relationship to modernity, the critique of mass society, and the political realm.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "For Arendt, the primary problem of the modern age in The Human Condition is world alienation rather than self-alienation.",
      "claim_type": "author_position",
      "supporting_excerpt": "ﻣﺴﺌﻠ، اﺻﻠﯽ دوران ﻣﺪرن، ﺑﺮ بۆ آرﻧﺖ »ﺟﻬﺎن از ﺑﯿﮕﺎﻧﮕﯽ« اﺳﺖ. او در ﮐﺘﺎب »وﺿﻊ ﺑﺸﺮ« ﺗﻮﺿﯿﺢ ﻣﯽدﻫﺪ ﮐﻪ ﺑﺤﺚ »ﺑﯿﮕﺎﻧﮕﯽ از ﺟﻬﺎن« و ﻧﻪ از ﺧﻮد ﺑﯿﮕﺎﻧﮕﯽ دارد ﺑﻪ ﻣﺸﺨﺼ، ة دوران ﻣﺪرن ﺗﺒﺪﯾﻞ ﻣﯽﺷﻮد",
      "support_kind": "direct",
      "confidence": 1
    },
    {
      "claim": "The first and most important consequence of world alienation is that humans lose the meaning of existence in the world and consequently their identity and understanding of reality.",
      "claim_type": "author_position",
      "supporting_excerpt": "اوﻟﯿﻦ و ﻣﻬﻢ، ﮥ ﺗﺮﯾﻦ ﻧﺘﯿﺠ، ﺑﺮاي آرﻧﺖ ﺑﯿﮕﺎﻧﮕﯽ از ﺟﻬﺎن اﯾﻦ اﺳﺖ ﮐﻪ ﻣﺎ ﻣﻌﻨﺎي وﺟﻮد و درﮐﻤﺎن از واﻗﻌﯿ، ﺗﺒﻊ آن ﻫﻮﯾﺖ ﻣﺎ و دﻫﯿﻢ و ﺑﻪ ﺧﻮد در زادﮔﺎه و در ﺟﻬﺎن را از دﺳﺖ ﻣﯽ",
      "support_kind": "direct",
      "confidence": 1
    }
  ],
  "definitions": [
    {
      "term": "ﺟﻬﺎن از ﺑﯿﮕﺎﻧﮕﯽ",
      "definition": "The condition in the modern era where people lose their connection to a shared common world and the understanding of the world, rather than merely experiencing self-alienation."
    }
  ],
  "distinctions": [
    {
      "item_a": "ﺑﯿﮕﺎﻧﮕﯽ از ﺟﻬﺎن",
      "item_b": "ﺑﯿﮕﺎﻧﮕﯽ از ﺧﻮد",
      "distinction": "Arendt argues that the defining characteristic of the modern age is world alienation (beiganagi az jahan) rather than self-alienation."
    }
  ],
  "references_to_other_sections": [],
  "unresolved_context": [
    "Connections between Arendt's world alienation and Plato's cave allegory, as well as totalitarianism and the banality of evil."
  ],
  "must_not_be_lost": [
    "Arendt's emphasis on world alienation as the central malady of modernity in The Human Condition."
  ]
}

